Registered a new unchecked track, coverage_target_contract_20260926, whose
spec records the contradiction: the coverage addon skips a file it cannot
instrument (coverage.gd:94-101, return false) but push_error escalates the
process exit code, and test_runner.py:484 turns any returncode > 1 into a
hard exit 2. The native runtime behaves the same way
(gd_tools_native_coverage.gd:92-95 plus the engine-error mapping), so this
is one decision applied twice, not two.

Records the verified trigger scope so the next session does not assume it is
common: plan_generator.py:501 invalidates the cache when files are deleted,
and plan_generator.py:138 raises if a file is missing at generation time, so
a generated plan cannot reference a nonexistent file. The realistic triggers
are a mid-run deletion, or a file that exists but does not compile.

Lays out three options (fail, skip silently, warn and continue) with the
trade-off table, recommends warn-and-continue because silently under-reported
coverage is the failure mode that survives review, and records four open
questions: which surfaces report the omission, whether --min compares against
the total or instrumented set, whether nonexistent and uncompilable paths
should diverge, and whether the coverage data schema needs a version bump.

Deliberately no plan.md: the chosen option determines the scope, so planning
before the decision would encode a guess.

